  <titlepage>
    <titlepage-items/>
  </titlepage>

  <preface>
    <p>
      A conventional autograded question keeps a verdict and discards the
      work. A drag-and-drop cardsort, fully logged, keeps the work: every
      placement of a card, every answer check together with its feedback,
      every reset, each stamped to the microsecond. Such a log carries two
      layers of information: an outcome layer about the answer a student 
      settled on, and a process layer about the path that produced it. What 
      the platform exports, however, is a flat table with one row per action. 
      The complete board appears only when the student checks; between checks 
      the state must be reconstructed; correctness is entangled with the 
      item's grading and feedback policy; and nothing in the table itself 
      says which inferences the data can support.
    </p>

    <p>
      This document builds the bridge from that export to defensible
      measurement, one layer at a time. A state model represents a single
      board snapshot exactly, as a configuration and a membership matrix. An
      event model gives each logged action a precise effect on the state, so
      that a working session becomes a timestamped trajectory through a
      configuration space. A processing pipeline replays raw rows into
      reconstructed trajectories, validates the replay against the
      platform's own check records, and attaches quality-control flags so
      that problems are marked rather than silently repaired. A feature
      layer then measures the outcome features for the submitted answer, 
      process features for the path, and cohort features for variation 
      across students. Next, a modeling layer places those measures in the 
      nested reality of students, items, and semesters. Throughout, raw data, 
      reconstructed state, and derived features are kept strictly separate, 
      and a single small sorting question about shapes anchors every 
      abstraction with worked numbers. The closing chapter draws the boundary 
      the framework depends on: what these measurements support directly, and 
      what they do not.
    </p>

    <p>
      The intended reader works with course interaction data and wants
      features that can be defended rather than merely computed. The
      mathematics asks only for comfort with vectors and matrices; no prior
      acquaintance with Runestone or its DataShop exports is assumed. The
      chapters run from the concrete to the formal to the operational to the
      inferential, and the notation appendix and glossary in the backmatter
      support reading them out of order.
    </p>
  </preface>

</frontmatter>
